{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-11-10 20:15:32 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-11 03:15:32 ICT\n\nReviewed `8cd9fc92..778747ce` statically. All line references below are at `778747ce`. No files edited; no suites rerun.\n\n1. **P1 must-fix — retained tokens are source-readable and transferable.**  \n   `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1546` inserts the compiler token into the map subsequently bound directly to the catch variable at `:1142`. The public escape at `:1314` authenticates only that token, without binding it to the original condition.\n\n   After catching a genuine condition, source can throw an unrelated map:\n   ```clojure\n   (throw {:yang.call/reason :yang.call/unsupported-binding\n           :yang.call/data {:forged true}\n           :dsg__condition (:dsg__condition e)})\n   ```\n   It passes authentication and escapes as a protocol condition rather than a fresh thrown payload. Reserved-symbol checks do not prohibit this keyword lookup. Token equality therefore fixes literal-`true` forgery but does **not** satisfy R5.\n\n   **Disposition:** bind provenance to the retained condition through an opaque association or a witness authenticating its complete contents. Add token-transplant and modified-condition regressions.\n\n2. **P1 must-fix — ordinary caught exceptions now expose the envelope instead of the original exception.**  \n   `src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1536` includes `:yang.call/thrown` in the whole-condition retention branch at `:1545`. Catch lowering still exposes that whole map (`:1142`), while `rewrite-ex-info` still implements exception accessors as direct `:yin.k/message`/`:yin.k/data` lookups (`:915`).\n\n   Consequently, this public-call path statically yields `[nil nil]` instead of `[\"boom\" {:x 1}]`:\n   ```clojure\n   (let [f (fn [] (throw (ex-info \"boom\" {:x 1})))]\n     (try (f)\n          (catch Exception e [(ex-message e) (ex-data e)])))\n   ```\n   Direct intra-unit th